{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7f408b54f706070c", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In return, the insurer agrees to make periodic income payments to you beginning immediately or at some future date.", "question": null, "answer": "An annuity is a contract with an insurance company. You pay for it with a lump sum or a series of payments, and in return the insurer agrees to make periodic income payments, either right away or at a future date. Annuities can also offer tax-deferred growth, but costs, risks, and features vary by contract.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4624, "output_tokens": 216}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7f408b54f706070c", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With a deferred annuity, you make a single payment or flexible payments over time that allow you to accumulate money for future income.", "question": "How does a deferred annuity differ from an immediate annuity in when it is used for income?", "answer": "A deferred annuity lets you build up money first for income payments in the future. An immediate annuity is typically purchased with a single payment, and income payments usually begin within one year.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4624, "output_tokens": 164}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7f408b54f706070c", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "However, unlike a fixed index annuity, you can lose money in a RILA.", "question": "Misconception: Annuities always guarantee that I cannot lose money.", "answer": "That is not true for every annuity. The source says you can lose money in a registered index-linked annuity (RILA), and variable annuities can also have losses. The potential for loss depends on the type of annuity and its contract features.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4624, "output_tokens": 266}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:141306db25a66280", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Immediate vs. Deferred Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With an immediate annuity, you make a single payment to purchase the annuity and typically start receiving income payments within one year of purchase. With a deferred annuity, you make a single payment or flexible payments over time that allow you to accumulate money for future income.", "question": null, "answer": "Immediate and deferred annuities differ mainly in when income payments begin. With an immediate annuity, you make a single purchase payment and typically begin receiving income within one year. With a deferred annuity, you make a single payment or flexible payments over time, and the money can accumulate before income payments begin. The time when the money accumulates is called the accumulation phase; when income payments begin, that is the payout phase.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 296}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:141306db25a66280", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Immediate vs. Deferred Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With an immediate annuity, you make a single payment to purchase the annuity and typically start receiving income payments within one year of purchase. With a deferred annuity, you make a single payment or flexible payments over time that allow you to accumulate money for future income.", "question": "How does an immediate annuity differ from a deferred annuity?", "answer": "With an immediate annuity, you make a single payment and typically begin receiving income payments within one year. A deferred annuity lets you make a single payment or flexible payments over time, building money for income payments at a future date.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 361}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:141306db25a66280", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Immediate vs. Deferred Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "With an immediate annuity, you make a single payment to purchase the annuity and typically start receiving income payments within one year of purchase. With a deferred annuity, you make a single payment or flexible payments over time that allow you to accumulate money for future income.", "question": "Misconception: An immediate annuity always starts paying income on the day you buy it, while a deferred annuity requires only one payment.", "answer": "An immediate annuity typically begins income payments within one year of purchase, not necessarily on the purchase date. A deferred annuity can be funded with a single payment or flexible payments over time, allowing money to accumulate before income payments begin.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 267}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:90d9bdf4ab1022bf", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Accumulation and Payout Phases", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "This time period during which your money is allowed to grow is called the accumulation phase. For both types of annuities, the period when income payments begin is called the payout phase.", "question": null, "answer": "Annuities can have two distinct phases. During the accumulation phase of a deferred annuity, money you pay into the contract is allowed to grow tax-deferred before you begin receiving income payments. The payout phase begins when those income payments start. In short: accumulation is the period before income payments begin; payout is the period when they begin.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4629, "output_tokens": 226}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:90d9bdf4ab1022bf", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Accumulation and Payout Phases", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "This time period during which your money is allowed to grow is called the accumulation phase. For both types of annuities, the period when income payments begin is called the payout phase.", "question": "What is the difference between the accumulation phase and the payout phase of an annuity?", "answer": "During the accumulation phase, money in the annuity is allowed to grow before income payments begin. The payout phase is the period when those income payments begin.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4629, "output_tokens": 237}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:90d9bdf4ab1022bf", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Accumulation and Payout Phases", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "This time period during which your money is allowed to grow is called the accumulation phase. For both types of annuities, the period when income payments begin is called the payout phase.", "question": "Misconception: The payout phase is the period when money is still accumulating before income payments start.", "answer": "Correction: The accumulation phase is when the annuity money is allowed to grow before income payments begin. The payout phase starts when income payments begin.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4629, "output_tokens": 260}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:de8511ebcd382cde", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Fixed annuities guarantee your money will earn at least a minimum interest rate during the accumulation phase.", "question": null, "answer": "A fixed annuity is a deferred annuity that guarantees your money will earn at least a minimum interest rate during the accumulation phase. It may earn more than that minimum, but only the minimum rate is guaranteed. The insurance company sets the rates.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 195}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:de8511ebcd382cde", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Fixed annuities guarantee your money will earn at least a minimum interest rate during the accumulation phase.", "question": "What interest-rate guarantee does a fixed annuity provide during its accumulation phase?", "answer": "It guarantees that your money will earn at least a minimum interest rate during the accumulation phase. It may earn more, but only the minimum rate is guaranteed; the insurance company sets the rates.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 192}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:de8511ebcd382cde", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Fixed annuities guarantee your money will earn at least a minimum interest rate during the accumulation phase. Fixed annuities may earn interest at a rate higher than the minimum but only the minimum rate is guaranteed.", "question": "Misconception: A fixed annuity guarantees that any interest rate it earns will stay fixed throughout the accumulation phase.", "answer": "Correction: A fixed annuity guarantees at least a minimum interest rate. It may earn more than that minimum, but only the minimum is guaranteed.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 225}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a27b2ca9a55538a0", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Indexed Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Fixed indexed annuities earn interest during the accumulation phase based, in part, on the performance of a specified benchmark (e.g., the S&P 500 Index) at the end of a specified term (e.g., one year).", "question": null, "answer": "A fixed indexed annuity is a deferred annuity whose interest during the accumulation phase is linked in part to the performance of a specified benchmark over a set term. The insurer credits interest at the term’s end, so you do not know the credited rate in advance. The credited rate is guaranteed not to fall below zero, even if the market declines, but limits may restrict how much you can earn. This means the annuity’s interest is not simply the same as the benchmark’s return.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 304}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a27b2ca9a55538a0", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Indexed Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The interest rate is guaranteed to never be less than zero, even if the market goes down.", "question": "How does a fixed indexed annuity determine interest, and what happens if the market goes down?", "answer": "During the accumulation phase, a fixed indexed annuity bases interest in part on the performance of a specified benchmark over a set term. The insurer credits interest at the end of that term, so you do not know the rate in advance. The credited interest rate will not be below zero even if the market goes down, though limits may restrict how much you earn.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 247}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a27b2ca9a55538a0", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Fixed Indexed Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The interest rate is guaranteed to never be less than zero, even if the market goes down.", "question": "Misconception: If the benchmark falls, a fixed indexed annuity must credit a negative interest rate and lose value from that decline.", "answer": "A fixed indexed annuity’s credited interest rate is guaranteed not to fall below zero, even when the market goes down. That does not mean the annuity has unlimited growth: the source notes that gains may be limited. Withdrawals or surrenders may also reduce value through charges.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 343}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e5818a38226f2f13", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Registered Index-Linked Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Registered index-linked annuities (RILAs) increase or decrease contract value during the accumulation phase based, in part, on the performance of a specified benchmark (e.g., the S&P 500 Index) at the end of a specified term (e.g., one year).", "question": null, "answer": "A registered index-linked annuity (RILA) is a deferred annuity whose value can rise or fall during the accumulation phase based partly on how a specified market index performs over a stated term. You do not know the interest rate in advance, and poor index performance can result in a loss. The insurer generally sets limits on both potential gains and losses, so a RILA can offer growth potential but also involves risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 290}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e5818a38226f2f13", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Registered Index-Linked Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "However, unlike a fixed index annuity, you can lose money in a RILA.", "question": "Can you lose money in a registered index-linked annuity (RILA), and how may its gains and losses be limited?", "answer": "Yes. A RILA’s contract value can decrease during the accumulation phase, including when poor index performance leads to negative credited interest. The insurer generally sets parameters that limit both gains and losses, although the source notes that some index-linked options may have unlimited risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 193}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e5818a38226f2f13", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Registered Index-Linked Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "However, unlike a fixed index annuity, you can lose money in a RILA.", "question": "Misconception: A registered index-linked annuity (RILA) cannot lose money when its linked index performs poorly.", "answer": "A RILA can lose money. Poor index performance may result in negative interest being credited, and the insurer may deduct money from the contract value. The insurer generally sets parameters that limit both gains and losses, but that does not eliminate the possibility of a loss.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 211}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:2d831ed032c35c9a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Variable Annuities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Variable annuities allow you, during the accumulation phase, to direct your contributions to a menu of different mutual funds. There are no limitations on losses or gains, instead, your return is based on the performance of the funds.", "question": null, "answer": "A variable annuity is a deferred annuity in which, during the accumulation phase, you choose how to direct your contributions among available mutual funds. Its return depends on how those selected funds perform, so both gains and losses are possible without a stated limit. Variable annuities also have explicit fees under the contract and fees for the underlying mutual funds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 237}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:2d831ed032c35c9a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Variable Annuities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "There are no limitations on losses or gains, instead, your return is based on the performance of the funds.", "question": "In a variable annuity, what determines your return during the accumulation phase?", "answer": "You choose from a menu of mutual funds for your contributions, and your return depends on how those selected funds perform. Unlike some other annuity types, variable annuities do not limit potential gains or losses.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 222}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:2d831ed032c35c9a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Variable Annuities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "There are no limitations on losses or gains, instead, your return is based on the performance of the funds.", "question": "Misconception: A variable annuity limits how much you can lose, so your investment is protected from poor mutual fund performance.", "answer": "Correction: Variable annuities do not limit losses or gains. Your return depends on the mutual funds you select, so poor fund performance can result in losses.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 244}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc8f59239ab5386f", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Retirement Income and Lifetime Payments", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "You can receive periodic payments for a specific amount of time. This income may be for the rest of your life, the life of your spouse or partner, or some other set period of time.", "question": null, "answer": "An annuity can help provide income in retirement. You make a lump-sum payment or a series of payments to an insurance company, and in return the insurer agrees to make periodic income payments. Depending on the contract, payments may begin immediately or at a future date and may last for a set period, your lifetime, or the lifetime of your spouse or partner. The payment terms and other features vary by contract, so review them carefully.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 258}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc8f59239ab5386f", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Retirement Income and Lifetime Payments", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "This income may be for the rest of your life, the life of your spouse or partner, or some other set period of time.", "question": "Can annuity income payments be arranged to last for a person’s lifetime?", "answer": "Yes. An annuity may provide periodic income payments for the rest of your life, for the life of your spouse or partner, or for another specified period, depending on the contract.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 174}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc8f59239ab5386f", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Retirement Income and Lifetime Payments", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "This income may be for the rest of your life, the life of your spouse or partner, or some other set period of time.", "question": "Misconception: Every annuity guarantees income for the rest of your life.", "answer": "Not necessarily. Annuity income may last for your lifetime, your spouse’s or partner’s lifetime, or another set period. The payment period depends on the annuity’s terms.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 213}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ea785002b513066a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Tax-Deferred Growth", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "You pay no taxes on any interest or investment gains in your annuity until you withdraw the money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid.", "question": null, "answer": "Tax-deferred growth means you do not pay taxes on interest or investment gains in an annuity while the money remains in it. Taxes may apply when you withdraw money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid. So “tax-deferred” means taxes are postponed, not that the growth is always tax-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 225}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ea785002b513066a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Tax-Deferred Growth", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "You pay no taxes on any interest or investment gains in your annuity until you withdraw the money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid.", "question": "What does tax-deferred growth mean for an annuity?", "answer": "It means you generally do not pay taxes on the annuity’s interest or investment gains while they remain in the annuity. Taxes apply when you withdraw the money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 212}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ea785002b513066a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Tax-Deferred Growth", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "You pay no taxes on any interest or investment gains in your annuity until you withdraw the money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid.", "question": "Misconception: Tax-deferred growth in an annuity means you never pay taxes on its interest or investment gains.", "answer": "Correction: Tax-deferred does not mean tax-free forever. The source says taxes on interest or investment gains are due when you withdraw money, receive income payments, or a death benefit is paid.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 204}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dfda191b389ad37", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Death Benefits", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "the person you name as your beneficiary receives at least the current value of the annuity, perhaps more.", "question": null, "answer": "An annuity death benefit is money payable to a named beneficiary if the annuity owner dies. For example, if the owner dies before income payments begin, the beneficiary receives at least the annuity’s current value, and some contracts may provide more. The amount and terms depend on the contract, and withdrawals can reduce the value of death benefits—sometimes by more than the amount withdrawn. Review the contract to understand how its benefit is calculated and paid.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 260}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dfda191b389ad37", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Death Benefits", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "If you die before you start receiving payments, the person you name as your beneficiary receives at least the current value of the annuity, perhaps more.", "question": "What may a beneficiary receive if the annuity owner dies before income payments begin?", "answer": "The named beneficiary receives at least the annuity’s current value, and possibly more.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 193}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dfda191b389ad37", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Death Benefits", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "If you die before you start receiving payments, the person you name as your beneficiary receives at least the current value of the annuity, perhaps more.", "question": "Misconception: An annuity’s death benefit always pays beneficiaries more than the annuity’s current value.", "answer": "Not necessarily. If you die before income payments begin, your beneficiary receives at least the annuity’s current value; the benefit may be higher, but the source does not say that a higher amount is guaranteed.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4626, "output_tokens": 370}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:f042977d2bc3d8d7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Insurance Company Credit Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "When considering purchasing an annuity, it is important to remember that an insurance company’s obligations under an annuity contract are subject to its financial strength and claims-paying ability.", "question": null, "answer": "Annuity payments depend on the insurance company’s ability to meet its contractual obligations. This creates insurance company credit risk: if the insurer has financial difficulties, it may not be able to pay you as promised. When assessing an annuity, consider the financial strength of the company offering it and understand that the contract’s promised payments depend on that company’s claims-paying ability.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 229}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:f042977d2bc3d8d7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Insurance Company Credit Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "if the insurance company has financial difficulties, it may not be able to pay you.", "question": "Why does an insurance company’s financial strength matter when you own an annuity?", "answer": "The insurer’s obligations under the annuity contract depend on its financial strength and claims-paying ability. If it has financial difficulties, it may not be able to make the payments it owes under the contract.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 172}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:f042977d2bc3d8d7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Insurance Company Credit Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "if the insurance company has financial difficulties, it may not be able to pay you.", "question": "Misconception: Once an annuity contract promises income payments, the insurer must pay them regardless of its financial condition.", "answer": "Annuity payments are not independent of the insurer’s ability to pay. The insurer’s obligations are subject to its financial strength and claims-paying ability, so financial difficulties may prevent it from making payments.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4625, "output_tokens": 202}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c757ac7dfbfea67", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Free Look Period", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "State law gives you a set number of days (usually 10 to 30 days) to change your mind about buying an annuity after you receive the annuity contract.", "question": null, "answer": "An annuity free look period is the time after you receive your contract when state law gives you a set number of days to change your mind about buying the annuity. The source says this period is usually 10 to 30 days. Check your contract for the exact length of your free look period and instructions for returning it.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 211}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c757ac7dfbfea67", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Free Look Period", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "State law gives you a set number of days (usually 10 to 30 days) to change your mind about buying an annuity after you receive the annuity contract.", "question": "What is an annuity free look period, and when does it begin?", "answer": "A free look period is the time allowed by state law for you to change your mind about buying an annuity. It begins after you receive the annuity contract and is usually 10 to 30 days. The contract should state the length of the period and how to return it.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 249}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c757ac7dfbfea67", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Free Look Period", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "State law gives you a set number of days (usually 10 to 30 days) to change your mind about buying an annuity after you receive the annuity contract.", "question": "Misconception: An annuity’s free look period lets me change my mind at any time.", "answer": "The free look period is limited: state law gives you a set number of days—usually 10 to 30 days—after you receive the annuity contract to change your mind. The contract should state the length of the period and how to return it.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 256}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d27df54a36a1a4f6", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Surrender Charges and Early Withdrawals", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Surrender charges will reduce the value of -- and the return on -- your investment.", "question": null, "answer": "A surrender charge is a fee that may apply when you withdraw some or all of an annuity’s value within a specified number of years after purchasing or contributing to it. The charge reduces the annuity’s value and your return, and these fees generally decrease over time. Early withdrawals may also have other consequences, such as taxes or tax penalties, so check your contract’s terms to understand what could apply.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 204}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d27df54a36a1a4f6", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Surrender Charges and Early Withdrawals", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "If you take a withdrawal of some or all of your contract value within a certain number of years of purchasing or contributing money to your annuity, the amount withdrawn may be subject to a surrender charge.", "question": "What may happen if you withdraw money from an annuity during its surrender-charge period?", "answer": "Some or all of the amount withdrawn may be subject to a surrender charge. The charge reduces the annuity’s value and the return on your investment.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 220}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d27df54a36a1a4f6", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Surrender Charges and Early Withdrawals", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "If you take a withdrawal of some or all of your contract value within a certain number of years of purchasing or contributing money to your annuity, the amount withdrawn may be subject to a surrender charge.", "question": "Misconception: A partial early withdrawal from an annuity avoids surrender charges because you are not withdrawing the entire contract value.", "answer": "A partial withdrawal may still be subject to a surrender charge. The charge can apply to some or all of the amount withdrawn if you take it within a specified number of years after purchasing or contributing to the annuity.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 229}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d45304b4f862d581", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Market Value and Interim Value Adjustments", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The insurer may also adjust the value of your annuity through a Market Value Adjustment or an Interim Value Adjustment. These contract adjustments are often negative and may significantly lower the value of your annuity. They are in addition to any surrender charge that applies.", "question": null, "answer": "A Market Value Adjustment (MVA) or Interim Value Adjustment (IVA) is a contract adjustment that can change an annuity’s value when you withdraw or transfer money before a specified period ends. The adjustment is often negative, so it may significantly reduce the amount you receive. It is separate from any surrender charge, which may also apply. Check your contract to understand when an adjustment could apply and how it may affect the value.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 362}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d45304b4f862d581", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Market Value and Interim Value Adjustments", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The insurer may also adjust the value of your annuity through a Market Value Adjustment or an Interim Value Adjustment. These contract adjustments are often negative and may significantly lower the value of your annuity. They are in addition to any surrender charge that applies.", "question": "How can a Market Value Adjustment or Interim Value Adjustment affect an annuity withdrawal?", "answer": "If you withdraw or transfer money before the end of a specified period, the insurer may adjust your contract value. These adjustments are often negative and can significantly reduce the value of your annuity. They are separate from, and may apply in addition to, any surrender charge.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 294}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d45304b4f862d581", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Market Value and Interim Value Adjustments", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These adjustments, also known as Interim Value Adjustments or Market Value Adjustments, are often negative and can result in significant loss in the value of your annuity. These adjustments are in addition to any surrender charge that may also be applicable.", "question": "Misconception: If an annuity has no surrender charge, an early withdrawal cannot reduce its value through a Market Value Adjustment or Interim Value Adjustment.", "answer": "A contract adjustment can be separate from a surrender charge. Some annuities may have a Market Value Adjustment or Interim Value Adjustment when money is withdrawn or transferred before a specified period ends. These adjustments are often negative, and may apply in addition to any surrender charge.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 385}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e9cf9a2074e97bb7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Exchanges and Conflicts of Interest", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Some financial professionals may have a financial incentive to offer you a new annuity in place of the one you own.", "question": null, "answer": "An annuity exchange means using the value of one annuity to buy another. The exchange may preserve tax deferral if you follow certain tax rules, but it is not automatically a better deal. A financial professional may have an incentive to recommend a new contract, so compare both contracts’ features, fees, rates, and risks before deciding. Also check for costs: leaving your current annuity may trigger a surrender charge, and the new contract may begin a new surrender-charge period.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4630, "output_tokens": 529}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e9cf9a2074e97bb7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Exchanges and Conflicts of Interest", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Some financial professionals may have a financial incentive to offer you a new annuity in place of the one you own.", "question": "What should you consider before exchanging one annuity for another, and why should you be alert to a possible conflict of interest?", "answer": "A financial professional may have a financial incentive to recommend a new annuity. Before exchanging, compare the features, fees, rates, and risks of both contracts to decide whether the new one is preferable to keeping your current contract. An exchange may also involve a surrender charge on the old annuity and start a new surrender-charge period on the new one.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4630, "output_tokens": 274}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:e9cf9a2074e97bb7", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuity Exchanges and Conflicts of Interest", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Some financial professionals may have a financial incentive to offer you a new annuity in place of the one you own. When exchanging your annuity, you may be subject to a surrender charge when exiting your old annuity.", "question": "Misconception: If a financial professional recommends exchanging your annuity, the new contract must be better for you.", "answer": "A recommendation alone does not establish that an exchange is preferable. A financial professional may have a financial incentive to suggest one, and leaving your current annuity may involve a surrender charge. Compare the features, fees, rates, and risks of both contracts before deciding.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4630, "output_tokens": 726}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e23eb971c660292", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities in Retirement Accounts", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "But if you are investing in an annuity through a tax-deferred retirement plan, you do not get any additional tax deferral. The annuity will be taxed like any other investment in the plan.", "question": null, "answer": "Annuities can offer tax-deferred growth during their accumulation phase. However, if you hold an annuity inside a tax-deferred retirement account, such as a traditional 401(k) or traditional IRA, it does not provide extra tax deferral beyond the account itself. The annuity is taxed like other investments in that plan. Consider how it fits into your overall financial situation.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 252}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e23eb971c660292", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities in Retirement Accounts", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "if you are investing in an annuity through a tax-deferred retirement plan, you do not get any additional tax deferral.", "question": "Does buying an annuity inside a tax-deferred retirement plan, such as a traditional 401(k) or traditional IRA, provide extra tax deferral?", "answer": "No. The annuity does not add tax deferral beyond what the tax-deferred plan already provides; it is taxed like other investments in the plan.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 192}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e23eb971c660292", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Annuities in Retirement Accounts", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "But if you are investing in an annuity through a tax-deferred retirement plan, you do not get any additional tax deferral.", "question": "Misconception: Buying an annuity inside a tax-deferred retirement plan, such as a traditional 401(k) or traditional IRA, gives you an extra layer of tax deferral.", "answer": "Correction: The source says an annuity in a tax-deferred retirement plan does not provide additional tax deferral; it is taxed like any other investment in the plan.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4627, "output_tokens": 239}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ec9a0a1c27a5650a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Explicit Fees vs. Implicit Costs", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Some annuities include implicit costs that are harder to identify, but also reduce the value of your annuity.", "question": null, "answer": "Annuity costs can be explicit or implicit. Explicit fees are taken directly from the annuity’s value and may appear on your account statement. Implicit costs are less visible: for example, the insurer may credit less interest than it earns or limit gains. Even without a separate bill, these implicit costs can reduce your return just as explicit fees do. Compare both fees and limits on performance when reviewing an annuity.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 230}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ec9a0a1c27a5650a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Explicit Fees vs. Implicit Costs", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "To pay for an annuity, the insurer may subtract some fees directly from your annuity’s value. These fees are called explicit fees. Some annuities include implicit costs that are harder to identify, but also reduce the value of your annuity.", "question": "How do explicit fees differ from implicit costs in an annuity?", "answer": "Explicit fees are deducted directly from the annuity’s value. Implicit costs are less obvious, but they can still reduce its value—for example, by lowering the interest credited or limiting potential gains.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 265}}
{"id": "a01613f4a8a24884:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ec9a0a1c27a5650a", "source_id": "a01613f4a8a24884", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities", "topic": "Annuities", "subtopic": "Explicit Fees vs. Implicit Costs", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These implicit costs can reduce your return in the same way that a direct or explicit fee would.", "question": "Misconception: If an annuity has no explicit ongoing fees, it has no costs that reduce its return. Is this true?", "answer": "No. Some annuities have implicit costs rather than explicit fees. For example, the insurer may credit less interest than it earns or limit gains. These costs can reduce your return just as an explicit fee can.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 4628, "output_tokens": 237}}
